CORRECTS AN EARLIER CLAIM IN ASSUMPTIONS.md. The entry 'commits name their files;
never a bare git commit' says '-o' protects you from sweeping *unstaged* work.
That is WRONG for a file another session is editing concurrently: 'git commit -o
-- <path>' commits that path's WORKING TREE content, staged or not. So -o
protects against sweeping *other paths*, and gives no protection at all within a
path two lanes both append to.

The only thing that actually works for a shared append-only file is to diff the
file immediately before committing and compare it to what you appended -- not
'git status', which shows only that it changed, not by whose hand.
